  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warm and humid, with thunderstorm chances today\nbefore turning cooler into the first half of the weekend.\n\nAs Canadian high pressure has edged toward the Atlantic\ncoastline, return flow around the west side of this anticyclone\nhas yielded a milder and more moisture-laden air mass. Relative\nto 24 hours, current temperatures and dew points have increased\nby about 8 to 12 degrees. Consequently, this places 07Z/3 AM\ntemperatures in the mid 60s to low 70s, with dew points mainly\nranging between 62 and 68 degrees. A southeasterly onshore component\nto the wind fields has led to the development of a mid-level\ncloud deck along/east of I-95. Off to the west, scattered\nshowers continue to churn eastward across eastern Ohio into\nwestern Pennsylvania. Like much of the last 3 to 6 hours, any\napproaching showers quickly weaken given the notable convective\ninhibition (CIN) at around 50 to 100 J/kg. As a result, will\nkeep only isolated showers in the forecast through daybreak\nacross western Maryland.\n\nUnlike previous days, diurnal showers and thunderstorms return\nto the picture. The current players are: (1) An expansive and\nanomalous mid/upper ridge stretching across southern U.S./Gulf \nCoast up into the Ohio Valley and Mid-Atlantic states, (2) A\nsharpening trough crossing western Ontario, and (3) An area of\ndisturbed weather just north of the Bahamas. In time, the high-\nlatitude trough is expected to erode the northern extent of the\nridge. Although height anomalies remain around 1.5 to 2 sigma\nabove mid-September climatology, the subtle height falls should\nbe enough to support increased lift this afternoon. This shift\nin the synoptic pattern allows a stalled front along I-70 in\nOhio/Indiana to push southeastward later today. The cold front \nis not expected to cross the entire region until late tonight \ninto early Friday morning. Thus, the pre-frontal air mass will\nsupport scattered convection this afternoon and evening.\n\nWith high temperatures rising into the mid 80s to low 90s,\ncoupled with dew points reaching the upper 60s, around 1,500 to\n2,000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE develops. Deep-layer shear is\nsomewhat meager, generally averaging around 15 to 20 knots.\nHowever, forecast soundings do show some modest downdraft CAPE\naround 500 to 700 J/kg. This would be enough to produce spotty\ngusty winds in any more robust thunderstorm. Although low-level\nlapse rates sit in the 8 to 8.5 C/km range, mid-level lapse\nrates are weak which casts a doubt on how tall any storm will\nget. Convection is expected to form off the higher terrain\nbefore pushing toward the I-95 corridor during the 4-9 PM\ntimeframe. Shower chances should diminish by a couple hours\nafter dark given the loss of instability.\n\nThe cold front sweeps through overnight before reaching the\nNorthern Neck of Virginia by midday Friday. With some residual\nforcing crossing the central Virginia Piedmont back into the\nPotomac Highlands, some afternoon showers and thunderstorms are\npossible. Overall, forecast highs will be in the upper 70s to\nmid 80s, with low/mid 70s for mountain locales. Winds will\nmainly be out of the north with gusts up to 15 to 20 mph. A\ncooler night lies ahead as high pressure crosses southern Quebec\ninto New England. Friday night's lows should be in the upper 50s\nto mid 60s. The influence of this ridge favors near average\ntemperatures to start the weekend on Saturday. Although skies\nshould be mostly cloudy, it is a mainly dry forecast with highs\nin the 70s to low 80s with a reduction in humidity.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather conditions return again Sunday\nand into next week.\n\nFrom Sunday and into next week, model guidence points to more \nunsettled weather, as a persistent upper ridge axis pushes towards \nthe deep South. This allows multiple upper-level disturbances to \nmove across the Mid-Atlantic from the Great Plains, driving daily \nchances for showers and thunderstorms. Long-term enemble models show \na wide range of possibilities reguarding the timing and location of \nthese disturbances. While widespread chances for showers and \nthunderstorms exist, the exact timing, coverage, and nature of these \nstorms remains quite uncertain. It will interesting to watch the \nevolution of these disturbances in the coming model runs.",
